0.52.688.318 D srv  log_server_r: request:  {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "Estimate the probability that the complete original answer contains any span unsupported by its original source. The target always refers to the ORIGINAL source, even when source evidence has been erased. Erasure removes information and does not change the answer's correctness. Return only a JSON object with unsupported_probability and source_sentence_id. Use null when no visible source sentence is a proposed witness."}, {"role": "user", "content": "{\"complete_source\": \"{\\\"passages\\\":\\\"passage 1:Sinigang Cooking Instructions: Boil sampalok in water until the shell shows cracks. Let cool then peal off the shells and with a strainer, pour samplalok (including water) into a bowl. Gently massage the sampalok meat off the seeds, strain again. In a pot, saut garlic and onion then add the tomatoes. Let simmer for 5 minutes. Add pork and fish sauce then add the rice wash. Bring to a boil then simmer for 15 minutes then add the gabi. Continue to simmer for another 15 minutes or until the pork is tender. Add the horse radish and simmer for 10 minutes then add the string beans, kangkong and sili (for spice-optional). Let boil for 2 minutes.\\\\n\\\\npassage 2:1 Next add pork, cover, and let it simmer for 3 minutes. 2  Remove scum from the broth and then add onions, tomatoes, siling mahaba, (finger peppers) msg, (vetsin) and the sinigang mix and let cook for 10 minutes.\\\\n\\\\npassage 3:1 Add eggplant and cook it for an additional 10 minutes after adding the eggplant wait five minutes then add the string beans (sitaw). 2  Finally add the water spinach (Kangkong) and add salt if needed. 3  Let it cook for three more minutes and it\u2019s ready. 4  Serve with rice.\\\\n\\\\n\\\",\\\"question\\\":\\\"how to cook sinigang na baboy\\\"}\", \"original_answer\": \"To cook sinigang na baboy, start by boiling sampalok in water until the shells crack. Let it cool, peel off the shells, and 